EXT. DRIFTING ICE FLOE, ARCTIC INLET - DAY

A white plain under a white sky. A scatter of gear: a collapsed tent, a sled, a battered handheld radio. Wind pushes snow in thin ribbons across the surface.

A FIGURE in a red parka kneels over a camp stove, back to us. Small. Methodical.

The figure turns. HANNELORE "HANNE" SKJOLD (40s, glacier goggles pushed up, frostbitten cheeks, a field biologist's patience in her face) pours melted water into a tin cup. She drinks. Looks at the horizon.

HANNE
(to herself)
Day nine.

She sets down the cup. Picks up the radio. Thumbs it.

HANNE (CONT'D)
Station, this is Skjold. Do you copy?

Static. She waits. Counts to thirty. Tries again.

HANNE (CONT'D)
Station, this is Skjold. Floe drift continues northeast. Rations, six days. Morale, fine.

She almost smiles at that last word.

Static, then a thin voice, far away.

STATION (V.O.)
...Skjold, copy... say again...

Hanne grips the radio.

HANNE
Station, I read you. Position unchanged. I am not in distress.

STATION (V.O.)
...hold... helicopter... three days...

HANNE
Understood. Three days.

She lowers the radio. Doesn't celebrate. Doesn't sag. Reaches for a notebook and a pencil stub.

The camera finds the page. A tally of marks, nine neat strokes. She adds a tenth.

Under the tally, in block letters: DON'T LOSE THE PENCIL.

HANNE (CONT'D)
(reading it aloud)
Don't lose the pencil.

She tucks the pencil behind her ear. Looks out at the ice, and the ice looks back, patient and enormous.

A low groan rolls under the floe. A thin crack runs a few meters away and stops.

SKJOLD
(mildly)
Not yet, thank you.

She zips the radio into her parka and starts to repack the sled.
